Question 13,14.
Explain in your own words (3-4 sentences) how splitting utility functions into separate files using imports and exports would help you scale a Zomato-like app as it grows from 2 to 20 features. Give one concrete example.

Answer:
Splitting utility functions into separate files using imports and exports makes the code more organized, reusable, and easier to maintain as the app grows. Instead of keeping all functions in one large file, each feature can have its own utility file that can be updated independently without affecting other parts of the application. For example, in a Zomato-like app, payment functions like calculateBill() and applyCoupon() can be stored in paymentUtils.js and imported wherever payment processing is needed. This approach helps a team manage 20 features more efficiently because developers can work on different modules without creating conflicts.





